/* book exam traps: Treating work as a state function because a reversible path lets you calculate it from P and V. Reversible only means P is defined along the path; it does not make work path-independent. | Thinking all reversible paths between two states give the same work. Different reversible P–V routes have different areas under them. | Writing dW or ΔW for a small or total amount of work. Use δW for a small path-dependent amount and W for total work. | Using W = ∫ P dV for an irreversible sudden expansion. Use W = Pext(V₂ − V₁) when Pext is fixed and finite, and remember Pext may not equal the gas pressure. | Mixing up exact and inexact differentials: ∮ dX = 0 for a state function X, but ∮ δW is generally not zero. */
